<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Djoumbe Fatima</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1836/37–<a href="/years/1878">1878</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Fatima bint Abderremane</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Djoumbe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima</a> was queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moheli">Mohéli</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comoro-islands">Comoros</a>, succeeding her father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanetaka">Ramanetaka</a>, a Merina prince, as a child in 1842. Her reign was marked by rivalry between French and British interests, and she abdicated in favour of her son in 1867, returning to power for a time later. Her dealings with foreign powers led towards French control of the island.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/djoumbe-fatima.jpg" alt="Djoumbe Fatima" width="320" height="562" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoine Trinquart · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Djoumbe_Fatima_1868.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
